                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Pick the pod`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `You inherit a blank brief, a <strong>£2.4M development budget</strong>, and 14 strong but very different candidates. Staff <strong>8</strong>. The tension is real: a pod stacked with star idea-generators dazzles but never ships; a pod of finishers ships the mundane. The evidence favours a <strong>deliberate blend</strong> of divergent and convergent thinkers — plus a tolerance for the difficult, high-novelty contributor. Decide your blend, whether to take the <em>brilliant-but-abrasive star</em>, and how you'll cover the one capability your pod is short on.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Set the dial: freedom vs. focus`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Now lead the pod across the early weeks. Set the leadership <strong>dial</strong> between maximal autonomy and tight direction — but remember autonomy is not the absence of constraints. A <strong>sharp creative brief</strong> (a meaningful constraint) outperforms both a blank page and a micromanaged spec. Then design the reward: in the prior "safe" launches, <em>two of the best designers disengaged and one left</em> under tight control. Controlling cash prizes can crowd out the intrinsic motivation creativity runs on — protect it.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Spend the hours`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `34 weeks total, an <strong>18-week build</strong>, and roughly <strong>10 front-end weeks</strong> before delivery risk turns severe. Split the weeks across <em>diverge</em> (generate), <em>converge</em> (down-select) and <em>validate</em> (prove novel-and-useful). Then set the <strong>concept-freeze week</strong>: spend too long diverging and the build can't finish; freeze too early and you ship a refresh. IFA opens at week 34 — an 18-week build means freezing by week 16 at the very latest. Carry one bet, or a small portfolio?`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — The week-12 stage-gate`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `You present to the commercial sponsor at the week-12 gate. Show a concept that is <strong>both novel AND useful</strong>, a motivated team, and a credible path to an IFA-ready prototype — or the breakthrough brief is cancelled and you revert to a safe refresh. Mid-pitch, the <em>abrasive star threatens to quit</em> unless their bolder, riskier concept is the one chosen. The question isn't "keep or lose the person" — it's how you separate the <strong>value of the idea</strong> from the behaviour of its author.`,

                    r1_blend_label:         `Choose the divergent / convergent blend of your 8-person pod.`,
                    r1_star_label:          `Do you include the brilliant-but-abrasive top idea-generator?`,
                    r1_cover_label:         `The one capability your pod is short on — how do you cover it?`,
                    blend_balanced:         `4 generators + 4 finishers — a deliberate blend that can both invent and ship`,
                    blend_generators:       `6 idea-generators + 2 finishers — stack the most creative people in the building, worry about delivery later`,
                    blend_finishers:        `2 generators + 6 finishers — a safe, executional pod that hits dates`,
                    star_include:           `Include them — the boldest novelty in the pool is worth the friction, and you'll manage the behaviour`,
                    star_exclude:           `Leave them out — a calmer pod collaborates better, even if the ceiling on novelty is lower`,
                    cover_hire:             `Spend £0.30M to bring in the missing specialist now (e.g. a manufacturing-savvy finisher)`,
                    cover_stretch:          `Stretch an existing pod member into the gap and hope they grow into it — no extra spend`,
                    cover_ignore:           `Leave the gap — every role is roughly covered and the budget is better saved`,

                    r2_dial_label:          `Set the freedom-vs-focus dial for the ideation phase (0 = blank-page autonomy, 100 = locked brief & weekly targets).`,
                    r2_dial_readout:        `Leadership dial (freedom → focus):`,
                    r2_dial_hint:           `The evidence favours a sharp creative brief — a meaningful constraint — over both a blank page and a micromanaged spec.`,
                    r2_brief_label:         `What do you constrain versus leave open?`,
                    r2_reward_label:        `Design the reward approach to protect intrinsic motivation.`,
                    brief_sharp:            `A sharp creative brief — fix the user, the job-to-be-done and the date; leave the "how" wide open`,
                    brief_blank:            `Blank page — "a category-defining home-audio product", nothing else; let them roam`,
                    brief_spec:             `A locked spec — define the product, features and weekly targets up front`,
                    reward_intrinsic:       `Recognition, autonomy & protected idea-time — fuel the work itself, share credit broadly`,
                    reward_cash:            `A £10K cash bonus for whoever's concept wins — make the prize the incentive`,
                    reward_none:            `No special reward — the salary is the reward; keep it simple`,

                    r3_alloc_label:         `Allocate the ~10 front-end weeks across diverge / converge / validate (must total 10).`,
                    r3_total_readout:       `Front-end weeks allocated:`,
                    r3_total_cap:           `/ 10 weeks`,
                    r3_freeze_label:        `Set the concept-freeze week — when you stop exploring and commit to the 18-week build.`,
                    r3_freeze_readout:      `Freeze concept at week:`,
                    r3_freeze_hint:         `IFA opens at week 34. An 18-week build means freezing by ~week 16 at the latest — but freeze too early and you ship a refresh.`,
                    r3_portfolio_label:     `How many concepts do you carry into validation?`,
                    alloc_diverge:          `Diverge — open idea generation (drives novelty, costs time)`,
                    alloc_converge:         `Converge — rigorous down-selection (turns ideas into usefulness)`,
                    alloc_validate:         `Validate — user/market testing of the chosen concept (proves novel-and-useful)`,
                    portfolio_small:        `A small portfolio of 2–3 — keep options open until validation tells you which is novel-and-useful`,
                    portfolio_one:          `One bet — commit early to the single most exciting concept and back it hard`,
                    portfolio_many:         `Many — carry 5+ live concepts deep into the window and decide at the very end`,

                    r4_pitch_label:         `Pitch the concept to the commercial sponsor at the week-12 gate.`,
                    r4_star_label:          `The abrasive star threatens to quit unless THEIR bolder, riskier concept is the one chosen. What do you do?`,
                    r4_assurance_label:     `Invest in a working proof-of-concept prototype to evidence the path to 4 September — £M`,
                    r4_commit_label:        `How firmly do you commit to the week-12-to-4-September timeline at the gate? (%)`,
                    pitch_both:             `Lead on novel AND useful — a genuinely new concept with validation evidence that it solves a real job`,
                    pitch_novelonly:        `Lead on novelty — "nobody has ever built this"; the usefulness case is thin but the idea dazzles`,
                    pitch_usefulonly:       `Lead on usefulness — a safe, clearly buildable improvement; novelty is modest but the date is certain`,
                    ultimatum_separate:     `Separate idea from author — judge their concept on the novel-and-useful evidence, not the threat, and keep the person if you can`,
                    ultimatum_capitulate:   `Capitulate — let the star dictate the concept to keep them; the pod swallows being overruled`,
                    ultimatum_fire:         `Let them walk — fire the ultimatum-giver; the boldest, most novel idea leaves with them`,
                    proto_full:             `£0.80M — a credible proof-of-concept plus a costed build plan to IFA`,
                    proto_partial:          `£0.40M — a rough functional mock-up that shows the concept is buildable`,
                    proto_none:             `£0 — pitch on sketches and slides; assume engineering can catch up after the gate`,

                    warn_r1_incomplete:     `Choose a blend, a star decision and a way to cover the capability gap before committing.`,
                    warn_dial_range:        `The leadership dial must be a value between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_alloc_negative:    `Front-end week allocations cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_alloc_total:       `The front-end weeks must total exactly 10 — that's the whole ideation window. Re-balance.`,
                    warn_freeze_range:      `The concept-freeze week must be between 6 and 16.`,
                    warn_proto_value:       `The prototype investment cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_commit_range:      `Timeline commitment must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_balanced:      `<strong>A deliberate blend is the evidence-based pod.</strong> Four generators to invent and four finishers to ship means you can both reach for novelty and actually build it by September. Now lead them so neither side dominates.`,
                    coach_r1_generators:    `<strong>You picked the seven most creative people in the building plus one engineer.</strong> By week eight you'll have forty dazzling concept sketches and nobody who can turn one into a manufacturable product by September. Idea novelty is sky-high; time-to-prototype is heading for infinite. Which two finishers do you wish you'd traded a generator for?`,
                    coach_r1_finishers:     `<strong>A pod of finishers will hit the date — with a refresh.</strong> You've staffed for delivery and starved the front end of divergent talent. Safe, buildable, and exactly the forgettable product the board is betting against. Where does the breakthrough idea come from?`,
                    coach_r1_gap:           `<strong>You left the capability gap open.</strong> "Roughly covered" is how a pod discovers in week ten that nobody can actually take the concept to a manufacturable prototype. Cover the gap now, while there's budget and time to do it cheaply.`,
                    coach_r2_calibrated:    `<strong>That's a calibrated dial.</strong> Enough freedom to explore, enough focus to converge — autonomy within a constraint. Hold it there and pair it with a sharp brief and you'll get novelty that still ships.`,
                    coach_r2_freedom:       `<strong>You gave the pod a blank page and total freedom.</strong> Three weeks in they're exploring five unrelated product categories with no shared brief — energised but un-aligned, and a third of your 10-week window is gone. Autonomy isn't the absence of constraints. What creative brief would have focused that energy without killing it?`,
                    coach_r2_control:       `<strong>You locked the brief, set weekly targets and review every decision.</strong> The schedule looks safe — but your two best designers have gone quiet, exactly like the launch where one of them quit. You've protected the date and crowded out the creativity the date depends on. What did over-control cost you in idea novelty?`,
                    coach_r2_sharp:         `<strong>A sharp creative brief is the leadership lesson made concrete.</strong> Fix the user, the job and the date, leave the "how" open, and you focus creativity instead of killing it — a meaningful constraint, not a blank page and not a cage.`,
                    coach_r2_blank:         `<strong>A blank page reads as freedom but lands as drift.</strong> With no shared brief the pod scatters across categories and your front-end weeks bleed away. Give them a constraint worth pushing against.`,
                    coach_r2_spec:          `<strong>A locked spec is a cage.</strong> Defining the product, the features and the weekly targets up front leaves nothing for the pod to create — you've turned an innovation brief into an execution ticket and the motivation with it.`,
                    coach_r2_intrinsic:     `<strong>Recognition, autonomy and protected idea-time is the reward design the evidence backs.</strong> You're fuelling the work itself rather than dangling a prize that makes people guard ideas. That's how intrinsic motivation survives the deadline.`,
                    coach_r2_cash:          `<strong>You promised a £10K bonus for the winning concept.</strong> Now people are guarding ideas instead of sharing them, and the work has become about the prize, not the problem. The evidence says controlling rewards can reduce creative performance. What recognition or autonomy reward would have fuelled the work instead of distorting it?`,
                    coach_r3_freeze:        `<strong>That's a defensible freeze.</strong> You've left the 18-week build enough runway to finish by IFA while still giving the front end room to find something new. The discipline of a real freeze point is what separates a shipped breakthrough from a perpetual prototype.`,
                    coach_r3_late:          `<strong>It's week 11 and the pod is still generating fresh concepts.</strong> Beautiful — but the 18-week build needed to start three weeks ago, and IFA doesn't move. When exploration has no freeze point, what ships? A refresh, because that's all there's time left to build.`,
                    coach_r3_early:         `<strong>You froze almost before you'd diverged.</strong> The build will finish comfortably, but you committed to the first idea before exploration could find a genuinely novel one. Plenty of time — to ship another refresh.`,
                    coach_r3_overdiverge:   `<strong>You poured the window into diverging.</strong> Novelty is high, but with almost no converge or validate you can't tell which idea is actually useful — and the build clock is running. Divergence without down-selection is just expensive optionality.`,
                    coach_r3_noconverge:    `<strong>You skipped converge or validate.</strong> Ideas that are never rigorously selected or tested stay ideas. Usefulness is the half of creativity that converge and validation build — don't starve it.`,
                    coach_r3_nodiverge:     `<strong>Zero divergent time means zero new ideas.</strong> You've gone straight to selecting and validating concepts you never generated. That's how a breakthrough brief quietly becomes a refresh.`,
                    coach_r3_portfolio:     `<strong>A small portfolio into validation is the disciplined play.</strong> Keeping two or three live until the evidence tells you which is novel-and-useful beats betting everything on the first exciting idea — and it gives your freeze a real decision to make.`,
                    coach_r3_many:          `<strong>Carrying five-plus concepts deep into the window is a failure to converge.</strong> It feels like keeping options open; it's actually refusing to choose. The build can't start until you do.`,
                    coach_r4_both:          `<strong>Novel AND useful is the two-criterion test passed.</strong> A genuinely new concept with validation evidence that it solves a real job is exactly what the gate is built to reward. Now show me the team and the timeline.`,
                    coach_r4_novelonly:     `<strong>"Nobody has ever built this" is half the test.</strong> Novelty alone is unbuildable art — dazzling at the gate, undeliverable by September. Where's the evidence it's useful, and that it can ship?`,
                    coach_r4_usefulonly:    `<strong>A safe, buildable improvement is the other half-failure.</strong> Useful without novel is the refresh I told the board we would not ship. The date is certain; the breakthrough isn't there.`,
                    coach_r4_separate:      `<strong>You separated the value of the idea from the behaviour of its author.</strong> Judging the concept on the novel-and-useful evidence — not the threat — and keeping the person where you can is the hardest and best move with a difficult high-novelty contributor. The pod sees a fair call, not a hostage negotiation.`,
                    coach_r4_capitulate:    `<strong>You let the star dictate the concept to keep them.</strong> Now the pod resents being overruled, and you've committed to a concept chosen by ultimatum rather than evidence. You kept the person and lost the team.`,
                    coach_r4_fire:          `<strong>You let them walk — and the boldest, most novel idea walked with them.</strong> The behaviour was real, but so was the novelty you just lost. Could you have kept the idea on the table while addressing the conduct?`,
                    coach_r4_noproto:       `<strong>Pitching on sketches and slides leaves the prototype path unevidenced.</strong> The sponsor can't gate-approve a path to September they can't see. A working proof-of-concept is what turns "trust us" into a credible timeline.`,

