                    insight_gate:           `Gate passed: you brought a concept that is both novel and useful, a team still motivated, and a credible path to a 4 September prototype. The breakthrough brief survives — you turned creative potential into a shippable bet under a fixed date.`,
                    insight_refresh:        `Safe refresh: the concept is buildable but not new. You optimised usefulness and starved novelty — exactly the forgettable product the board bet against. The date was never the risk; the breakthrough was.`,
                    insight_art:            `Unbuildable art: the concept dazzles but can't ship. You optimised novelty alone and the usefulness — and the prototype — never came together. The gate fails the two-criterion test.`,
                    insight_short:          `The brief was cancelled: the gate needs novel AND useful AND a motivated team AND a credible prototype path, all at once. At least one was missing, so the sponsor reverted to a safe refresh.`,
                    insight_motivation:     `Team motivation fell below the line. Creativity is fuel-limited — an over-managed or cash-distorted pod stops generating the very novelty the bet needs, no matter how good the people are.`,
                    insight_cash:           `The £10K cash prize did what the evidence warns: people guarded ideas instead of sharing them, and the work became about the prize. Controlling rewards crowded out the intrinsic motivation creativity runs on.`,
                    insight_control:        `You over-controlled the pod. The schedule felt safe, but tight direction and a locked brief crowded out idea novelty — you protected the date and undercut the reason for the date.`,
                    insight_freeze:         `You never set a real freeze point. Each new concept felt more exciting, the 18-week build ran out of runway, and the only thing left to ship was a refresh. Time-boxing exploration is the discipline a fixed date demands.`,
                    insight_star:           `You lost the star contributor, and with them the top of your novelty range. Separating the value of a bold idea from a difficult author's behaviour is the skill that keeps the idea on the table.`,
                    insight_breakthrough:   `Breakthrough shipped: novel, useful, a motivated team, and a costed path to IFA — the rare result where a first-time leader turned a blank brief and a difficult pod into a category-defining product on a date that wouldn't move.`,

                    hint_freeze:            `You haven't committed to a freeze in time — the 18-week build can't finish. Pull the freeze week inside the window (by ~week 16) or you'll be forced into a refresh.`,
                    hint_control:           `Motivation is collapsing under tight control. Ease the dial off the focus extreme and trade the locked spec for a sharp creative brief — the date depends on the creativity you're crushing.`,
                    hint_cash:              `Your cash prize is distorting behaviour. Switch to recognition, autonomy and protected idea-time so the pod shares ideas instead of guarding them.`,
                    hint_art:               `Novelty is high but usefulness is low — you're heading for unbuildable art. Invest converge and validation time, and pitch the idea as both new AND buildable.`,
                    hint_refresh:           `Usefulness is high but novelty is low — that's a refresh, not a breakthrough. Protect divergent idea-time and don't freeze before exploration finds something genuinely new.`,
                    hint_proto:             `Prototype readiness is low with the gate approaching. Fund a working proof-of-concept and commit firmly to the timeline so the sponsor can see the path to September.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — novelty, usefulness, motivation and prototype readiness all healthy. Hold the line at the gate: pitch novel-and-useful and separate the star's idea from the ultimatum.`,

